{
  "section": "lmr-rl-reasoning",
  "addedResources": [],
  "units": [
    {
      "id": "generation-decision-process-rewards",
      "title": "Language generation as a decision process, and sequence, token, outcome and process rewards",
      "hours": 3,
      "target": [
        "explain"
      ],
      "topics": [
        "language-generation-mdp",
        "sequence-token-rewards"
      ],
      "outcomes": [
        "Specify the prompt distribution, token actions, prefix transitions and stopping events for a verifiable language task.",
        "Distinguish reward timing from reward meaning, and record what the verifier actually observes."
      ],
      "why": "An RL result is about a particular task and trajectory contract. Changing answer extraction or the stopping rule can change the measured gain without changing the model.",
      "next": [
        {
          "unit": "llm-research/lmr-rl-reasoning/language-policy-gradient-baselines",
          "note": "Derive the update for this trajectory contract."
        }
      ],
      "study": [
        {
          "resource": "cs336-a5-rlvr-lmr8",
          "title": "CS336 Spring 2026 Assignment 5: language trajectories",
          "url": "https://github.com/stanford-cs336/assignment5-alignment/blob/main/cs336_spring2026_assignment5_alignment.pdf",
          "locator": "Sections 4.1.1 to 4.1.3, Language models as policies, Trajectories, and Rewards and return"
        },
        {
          "resource": "rlhf-book-rl-lmr8",
          "title": "RLHF Book Chapter 6: Reinforcement Learning",
          "url": "https://rlhfbook.com/c/06-policy-gradients.html#related-mdp-vs.-bandit-framing",
          "locator": "Implementation, Related: MDP vs. Bandit Framing"
        },
        {
          "resource": "rlhf-book-rl-lmr8",
          "title": "RLHF Book Chapter 7: Reasoning and Inference-Time Scaling",
          "url": "https://rlhfbook.com/c/07-reasoning.html#the-role-of-rlvr",
          "locator": "The Role of RLVR: math-answer extraction, code-test rewards and the verifier loop"
        }
      ],
      "focus": [
        "Build on Trajectory objectives and the likelihood-ratio identity. For a single-turn task, the prompt is sampled externally; appending a generated token is the deterministic transition.",
        "Sequence versus token describes where reward attaches. Outcome versus process describes what it judges. A step reward can attach to the last token of a multi-token step.",
        "Record EOS, a stop string and a token-limit stop separately. A token limit does not imply a sampled EOS, and a multi-token stop string is not necessarily one action.",
        "Use one small task in `lmlab`, such as reversing short strings. Freeze train, validation and test prompt identities, the accepted output grammar and the independent exact-answer evaluator before collecting data."
      ],
      "reproduce": "Closed-book, write the trajectory contract for reversing `ab` to `ba`, with one character per token and a sampled EOS. List the three sampled actions and their prefixes. Put reward 1 only on the correct terminal answer and use \\(\\gamma=1\\): each action's reward-to-go is 1. Then place an additional process reward after the first character and show which returns change.",
      "traps": [
        "Calling deterministic prefix transitions a deterministic policy. The next token is still sampled.",
        "Treating a correct final answer as proof that every intermediate step is correct. The verifier may inspect only the answer.",
        "Dropping malformed or truncated outputs. Keep their outcome classes and costs even when the declared loss excludes them."
      ],
      "selfTest": [
        {
          "q": "Does broadcasting a terminal reward to every token create process supervision?",
          "a": "No. It assigns outcome credit through the estimator; no intermediate step has been checked."
        },
        {
          "q": "When do the bandit and token views describe the same expected reward?",
          "a": "When they induce the same distribution over completed responses and use the same terminal reward and stopping contract, without changing length weighting or discounting."
        },
        {
          "q": "What belongs in the state for a token action?",
          "a": "The prompt and all preceding generated tokens, plus any task state needed for the declared environment. The completed answer must not leak into an earlier prefix."
        }
      ],
      "practice": [],
      "practiceNote": "The section mini-project covers this: the versioned task, split, output grammar, terminal-event and reward-placement contract for the tiny RL study.",
      "stopWhen": "Your task contract assigns a state, action mask, stopping reason and reward to a correct, incorrect, malformed and truncated response without ambiguity.",
      "fit": {
        "verdict": "kept",
        "note": "Kept: the assignment defines trajectories and terminal rewards; the book supplies the bandit and process-feedback distinctions. The mini-project applies them to one versioned task."
      }
    },
